,701,582,686,990,279,9544,20855,57817,13,576,9544,20855,57817,5302,429,1447,59,9640,11520,26888,90,17,92,488,1124,26888,58,18,15370,18,5410,47822,16,17,92,284,1124,1242,15159,74,28,15,92,47822,16,17,92,1124,6863,316,90,16,17,15170,74,92,19788,26888,90,17,5410,47822,16,17,12646,92,19788,26888,58,18,15370,18,5410,61,74,198,59,2533,1654,1184,311,8253,892,3793,304,419,14461,525,24438,13,362,4647,374,24438,421,2176,1124,11520,26888,90,17,11035,8,323,1124,11520,26888,58,18,15370,18,11035,8,525,9226,311,7546,13430,429,1102,304,24438,5109,13,44763,11,1124,1188,59,26888,90,17,5410,47822,16,17,12646,11035,8,374,24438,421,17767,16,17,12646,57758,374,1496,11,323,1124,1188,59,26888,58,18,15370,18,5410,61,74,57758,374,24438,421,17767,74,57758,374,264,5248,315,220,18,13,15277,11,17767,74,57758,1969,26553,2176,4682,25,17767,74,57758,1969,387,264,5248,315,220,18,323,17767,16,17,12646,57758,1969,387,1496,382,10061,594,1140,279,3204,2750,315,17767,74,59,1648,1406,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of\n\\[(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}.\\]\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo find the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\), we will use the binomial theorem. The binomial theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = \\sum_{k=0}^{12} \\binom{12}{k} (\\sqrt{2})^{12-k} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\n\\]\n\nWe need to determine which terms in this expansion are rational. A term is rational if both \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(\\sqrt[3]{3}\\) are raised to integer powers that result in rational numbers. Specifically, \\((\\sqrt{2})^{12-k}\\) is rational if \\(12-k\\) is even, and \\((\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\\) is rational if \\(k\\) is a multiple of 3. Therefore, \\(k\\) must satisfy both conditions: \\(k\\) must be a multiple of 3 and \\(12-k\\) must be even.\n\nLet's list the possible values of \\(k\\):\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"4a4dc36e068012b42b44355da66bbc107a4af9fb1137c9227ef4d52ca240741f"},"seconds":41.0179443359375,"seed":695817121362541700,"stop":"eos","text":"\\(k\\) must be a multiple of 3 and \\(12-k\\) must be even. The possible values of \\(k\\) that satisfy these conditions are \\(0, 3, 6, 9, 12\\). We now examine each of these values:\n\n1. **For \\(k = 0\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\binom{12}{0} (\\sqrt{2})^{12} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^0 = 1 \\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{12} \\cdot 1 = 1 \\cdot 2^6 = 64\n   \\]\n\n2. **For \\(k = 3\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\binom{12}{3} (\\sqrt{2})^9 (\\sqrt[3]{3})^3 = \\binom{12}{3} (\\sqrt{2})^9 \\cdot 3 = \\binom{12}{3} \\cdot 2^{4.5} \\cdot 3 = \\binom{12}{3} \\cdot 2^4 \\cdot 2^{0.5} \\cdot 3\n   \\]\n   This term is not rational as it includes \\(2^{0.5} = \\sqrt{2}\\).\n\n3. **For \\(k = 6\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\binom{12}{6} (\\sqrt{2})^6 (\\sqrt[3]{3})^6 = \\binom{12}{6} \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 = \\binom{12}{6} \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 = 924 \\cdot 72 = 66528\n   \\]\n\n4. **For \\(k = 9\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\binom{12}{9} (\\sqrt{2})^3 (\\sqrt[3]{3})^9 = \\binom{12}{9} \\cdot 2^{1.5} \\cdot 3^3 = \\binom{12}{9} \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot 27\n   \\]\n   This term is not rational as it includes \\(\\sqrt{2}\\).\n\n5. **For \\(k = 12\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\binom{12}{12} (\\sqrt{2})^0 (\\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 3^4 = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 81 = 81\n   \\]\n\nThe rational terms are for \\(k = 0, 6, 12\\). Adding these terms together, we get:\n\\[\n64 + 66528 + 81 = 66673\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of all the rational terms is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{66673}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{66673}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"\\(k\\) must be a multiple of 3 and \\(12-k\\) must be even. The possible values of \\(k\\) that satisfy these conditions are \\(0, 3, 6, 9, 12\\). We now examine each of these values:\n\n1. **For \\(k = 0\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\binom{12}{0} (\\sqrt{2})^{12} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^0 = 1 \\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{12} \\cdot 1 = 1 \\cdot 2^6 = 64\n   \\]\n\n2. **For \\(k = 3\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\binom{12}{3} (\\sqrt{2})^9 (\\sqrt[3]{3})^3 = \\binom{12}{3} (\\sqrt{2})^9 \\cdot 3 = \\binom{12}{3} \\cdot 2^{4.5} \\cdot 3 = \\binom{12}{3} \\cdot 2^4 \\cdot 2^{0.5} \\cdot 3\n   \\]\n   This term is not rational as it includes \\(2^{0.5} = \\sqrt{2}\\).\n\n3. **For \\(k = 6\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\binom{12}{6} (\\sqrt{2})^6 (\\sqrt[3]{3})^6 = \\binom{12}{6} \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 = \\binom{12}{6} \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 = 924 \\cdot 72 = 66528\n   \\]\n\n4. **For \\(k = 9\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\binom{12}{9} (\\sqrt{2})^3 (\\sqrt[3]{3})^9 = \\binom{12}{9} \\cdot 2^{1.5} \\cdot 3^3 = \\binom{12}{9} \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot 27\n   \\]\n   This term is not rational as it includes \\(\\sqrt{2}\\).\n\n5. **For \\(k = 12\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\binom{12}{12} (\\sqrt{2})^0 (\\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 3^4 = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 81 = 81\n   \\]\n\nThe rational terms are for \\(k = 0, 6, 12\\). Adding these terms together, we get:\n\\[\n64 + 66528 + 81 = 66673\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of all the rational terms is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{66673}\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:445"}
